= Part XII. Dawah, Doubts and Conversion

== 64. Dealing with doubts

=== 64.1 Doubts, questions and levels of certainty
// NODE P15.C01.S01
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR304:1-62 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("I've gotten this question a few times during the past year. The question goes something along the lines of: \"I'm having doubts in [Islam], what am I supposed to do?\" or \"I keep watching YouTube videos against Islam, how do I make the doubts stop?\" Well, there are three approaches to this sort of thing. I believe the vast majority of people simply couldn't care less about information that conflicts with their beliefs. They just don't care, they shrug it off. But you're not that type of person, I'm guessing, because you seem to be the type that gets eaten up by this stuff. That's probably why you're here for answers. Then comes the third type of people, who are naturally inquisitive but won't let these things eat them and won't cause them doubts. Why? It's because they do the research and most of the time they do find the answers. If you happen to be in the second group, then you do want to be in the third. You will want to learn the Arabic language and you want to familiarize yourself with the classical sources instead of having to rely on secondhand information. You'll want to do these things because you're not the type that ignores arguments, you're naturally curious.", "I'm somewhat similar, by the way. I'm inquisitive and honestly I don't think I'd be exaggerating if I've said that I've watched and read more criticisms against Islam [than] you have. But here comes the question that you might be thinking of, which is: shouldn't I, Farid, be filled with doubts? No, not really. I'm a very open-minded person. I've been challenged hundreds of times and I've made changes to my ideology due to those challenges. Those that do know me will testify that I'm an open-minded, critical person, and yet I'm not drowning in doubt after countless hours of going through anti-islamic content. So what is [the secret]? Well, it's a simple one really: my foundations are [established] and due to that I'm not bothered by random arguments.",), "Farid Responds, “\"I'm having doubts in Islam... What do I do?\"”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4KxQtb6Jzno", ((25, "0:25–2:34"), (154, "2:34–4:01"),), id: 3161)
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML267:225-271 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("I think a lot of people use the term \"Doubt\" and they don't understand the term \"Doubt\". I do think this is a problem, that some people might think something is a doubt, or it's more of a question rather than a doubt. A Doubt is where you're not sure whether this thing is right or wrong. A Question is where you don't understand a specific thing, you're curious about what does this specific thing mean. So a lot of Muslims, they message me and say \"I have a doubt\", and what they mean by doubt is that they have a question: \"How do you explain a specific part of the Quran?\" That's not a doubt, that is a question: something that you've not fully understood, you do not know how to take it, how to perceive it, and then you want to ask that specific thing. That's fine. It's completely fine for someone to have a question.", "Doubt is problematic when it comes to the essential beliefs. You can not have a doubt about the essential things, like about God's existence, Prophet Muhammad being a Messenger of God, or the Quran being the Word of God. That can take you out of Islam. If you're not sure whether the Prophet is a Messenger or not, you're not a Muslim. You can not be a Muslim without being sure that he's a Messenger of God. You can not be a Muslim without knowing the Quran IS from God. You can not be a Muslim without believing in God. So, having a question about it is different than to doubt whether He exists or He doesn't exist.", "But also, certainty and doubt are three levels. The first one is to believe it in your heart, which is from a theoretical point of view, from a rationality point of view. This is the first level that you need to achieve: you're convinced rationally that this is from God. There's a second level, which is actually to see it, to have certainty by seeing that thing. And the third level is to actually experience it. So we have actual Prophets of God who already had the first level but they were asking for the second level. For example, Moses, where he said (Ala'raf7:143) رَبِّ أَرِنِىٓ أَنظُرْ إِلَيْكَ (Oh my Lord I want to see you). He is already communicating with God directly, actually SPEAKING with God, but he wants to move to the next level where he sees the Creator. And then Allah responds that you're not going to be able to bear it in this life. Your eyesight is not able to bear it (Ala'raf7:143).", "Also Abraham, he asked for something, that he wanted the next level of certainty, where God turns a bird from clay into an actual living bird and he sees it. After he cuts and puts a part on every Mountain, it forms into a bird and it actually flies and he sees that with his own eyes (Albaqara2:260). That's the next level, where you're actually seeing these things. It's the next level of Certainty. But the first level of Certainty, which is to be sure, be convinced, without a shadow of a doubt that the Quran is from Allah, that Allah is the Creator, and Prophet Muhammad is a Messenger, you have to have it to be a Muslim.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “American Questions Muslim On Free Will In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6uEKa9ZO9u4", ((740, "12:20–12:57"), (782, "13:02–13:20"), (806, "13:26–14:07"), (853, "14:13–14:40"),), id: 3162)
