#passage(("You cannot have doubt unless you actually have an answer to what that doubt is. If someone says he has a doubt: Ok, what is the doubt? That's not doubt, that's fear. You'll have an actual reasoning, argumentation: \"Ok, here's my doubt, this is my problem, can you help me fix this issue?\" This is what the doubt is.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Omnist Left Speechless After Hearing This Muslim's Answer! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yVHQD2W5S0c", ((2691, "44:51–45:15"),), id: 3163)
// U ML084:817-903 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("You remove them by becoming a Muslim. The minute you become a Muslim, Allah forgives all the sins that you've done in the past 39 years or 40 years. They're all gone. That is what Islam teaches.", "If you be skeptical, you need to have reasons to be skeptical. If there is overwhelming evidence that something is true, then skepticism is not valid, unless it's based on proof. You can be afraid, but this is why you understand that you're doing something for the sake of God. If you're doing something for the sake of God, then who do you fear? Who is stronger than God? Why would you be afraid? This is why we do the research. Why would you be afraid when you only find the evidence in one place? There is no evidence that is in another place, then why are you afraid? There is rational fear and there is irrational fear. We need to look at our fear and understand. So if your rational fear is Hell, then you would obey the evidence that leads you to God. You wouldn't do the opposite.", "You worship that God after establishing with evidence that He's God. So you cannot fear that He's the wrong God when you've already established that He's the right God. You ONLY worship after realising that this is the Truth. How are you going to fear when you already established that this is the Truth? If your questions were answered, and the evidences have been given to you, then where is the rational fear?", "There are foundational questions, and not foundational questions. Foundational questions is the ones that would establish this is from God. Once this is established, it is from God. Every Muslim has questions. The criteria is not answering every question you have. The criteria is answering the foundational question of whether this is from God or not. There is an exercise of us asking questions that are not arriving to a point, because you already asked the most important question, which is, \"Why is Islam the Truth?\" And I've given you the reason. So if there are reasons that Islam is the Truth, like what Prophet Muhammad did, and he couldn't have done except if he was a Messenger of God, then I have no excuse to delay accepting the Truth.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Wife Grills A Muslim With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tUX4xfgST_E", ((2472, "41:12–41:22"), (2492, "41:32–42:51"), (2582, "43:02–44:08"), (2658, "44:18–44:59"),), id: 3164)

=== 64.2 Building a foundation before facing doubts
// NODE P15.C01.S02
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR304:63-122 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("Imagine your best friend, who couldn't hurt a fly, was convicted of a crime. You know for a fact that there was some sort of mistake. If there was a witness, you would simply believe that the witness was mistaken. If there was DNA evidence that your friend was at the crime scene, you would accept that your friend was there, but perhaps due to some sort of coincidence. You would need overwhelming evidence to make you change your mind, since it is established in your heart that your friend could never do such a thing. The same applies to any set of beliefs. Your religion may be a fortress that cannot be shaken, or it could be a house of cards that falls at the sight of a single allegation.", "Let me share with you an example of a fortress. During the sixth year after the migration to Medina, the Prophet, peace be upon him, and his companions traveled to Mecca with the intention of visiting the Kaaba to perform [Umrah]. When they got close, the Meccans prevented them from entering the city. Instead a peace treaty was signed and the Muslims had to return back to Medina. Naturally, the Muslims were very disappointed by the outcome. However, was this seen as a false prophecy? For those whose faith hangs on a [thread], this may have been sufficient for Islam to be considered false. However, to a Muslim whose faith is built like a fortress, like the Sahaba, the event was disappointing but ultimately there was nothing more than just a hiccup. There were no cases of apostasy, and the Muslims returned to Mecca and performed their rituals the very next year.", "This is the mentality that you should have. Build that fortress. Learn why Islam is true. Study the proofs for Islam. Is the evidence for Islam and for the Prophet, peace be upon him, sufficient? When you are at that stage, you will stop caring about the petty evidence against Islam. You won't feel the need to binge-watch criticisms against the religion. Now, I'm not telling you to stop being a critical person. However, your mentality will change. If you stumble upon a question that you cannot answer, you will realize that it isn't necessary for you to know what the answer is now, since you're aware that a good answer exists elsewhere.",), "Farid Responds, “\"I'm having doubts in Islam... What do I do?\"”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4KxQtb6Jzno", ((246, "4:06–5:09"), (309, "5:09–6:27"), (387, "6:27–7:39"),), id: 3165)
