=== 64.4 Seeking answers from people of knowledge
// NODE P15.C01.S04
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR178:1-76 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("The dumbest thing that you can do when you have a doubt: it's leaving it in the comments or putting it in any public space, whether it's social media or forums or anything online for the public to see. That's the dumbest thing that you can do if you have a doubt. And the reason is because you can probably find an answer in the next couple of days, but guess what? That little question that you had, that was bugging you perhaps slightly, is now bugging someone a lot. Perhaps may push someone away from the [deen]. Perhaps it could cause someone to fall into apostasy. Why? It's because you felt like putting that up online for the public to consume. And the reason you did that is because you're looking for an answer. But you can find an answer by contacting someone privately, by talking to a [sheikh], by reading up books on the subject, by googling it. And the vast majority of arguments out there against the [deen], you can find good answers to just by running a quick google search.", "Whenever you come across a channel that has shubahat that you find frustrating, that you don't have an answer to, you know what's the worst thing you could do? You could introduce an audience that's not aware of that channel to that channel, by saying, \"Hey, can someone respond to this obscure channel that no one knows about?\" Now you see, you're bringing Muslim audience to that channel. That's a problem. So when there's a specific issue that you have, you contact someone privately: contact the [sheikh] privately, contact a friend privately, contact me privately. Worst possible scenario, it's going to cause bigger issues to someone else.",), "Farid Responds, “If you have a doubt DON'T do this!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9sH7m8-zqmc", ((9, "0:09–1:35"), (98, "1:38–2:34"),), id: 3174)
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML121:808-945 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("It's not about whether it is clear to you or not. It's what the tradition says. Tradition already explicitly mentions in many place, like I already told you, there are two types of knowledge. So it's not a new category or something. The Quran already mentions that, and then I already told you the Quran says that Allah knows everything, meaning before it happens. And then I told you that our scholars already mentioned this and have a specific term for it. It's not something that we're bringing out of our pockets or something; something that is tracing all the way back to the companions, which means the prophet has taught them the meaning of these verses, that this knowledge here is referring to that. That's why you didn't get the disbelievers of Mecca saying, \"Oh, how does God not know?\", because they understood the Arabic language and they understood there's two types of knowledge. We didn't get this criticism because it's not a real criticism in any way, shape or form.", "You cannot say he doesn't know it now because he knows it will happen. That's why it doesn't use the term \"doesn't know\". But it's now referring to a different type of knowledge, which is the knowledge of something appearing and happening after it happened. Our scholars already explained this in the book, that these verses are referring to this. You didn't get people coming and saying, \"Oh, Allah doesn't know something and he will know something in the future.\" Nobody had understood the verses like this.", "We're not talking about the translation. You cannot appeal to the wrong translations once the explanation is given, because I already gave you also [Lane's] lexicon, which is not my interpretation of the Arabic language; it's already telling you how these words are used. So there is no other interpretation. That is how everyone understand it. That's why nobody have struggled with this verse, from Muslims who read the Quran and read surah [Al-Kahf]. Every Friday you have them reading it in the mosque. Nobody understands it that way, because they understand there's a story being told here, his journey, what he's doing, and him here seeing the sun. When you hear the story you visualize, which is the eloquence of the Quran. I can already visualize what he was doing. He was walking. I can visualize the lake. I can visualize the sun setting. I can visualize him seeing the people. So that beautiful eloquence of the Quran, somehow someone is coming: \"Oh, there's an issue.\" There's no issues. The issue of the understanding, comprehension of the person, nothing else, because we've been reading these verses, never had any issues with them.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Confronts Muslim On Mistakes Found In The Quran! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L-Zd3evV1oY", ((2662, "44:22–45:13"), (2750, "45:50–46:35"), (2879, "47:59–49:12"),), id: 3175)
