#passage(("What does the Quran tell you to do when you as a layman don't understand something? Ask people that know. So why are you blaming the Quran when the Quran already gave you the fixed solution for these issues when you got confused? You're saying you got confused: \"Oh, I looked at this verse. I looked at this,\" which, you don't speak the Arabic language. You don't understand the primary text and literature. You're a layman. You don't understand. Go ask the people of knowledge if you do not know. Quran is explicitly clear about that in chapter 16 and other places. So when the Quran tells you to do that, you didn't do that. So who's to blame? The Quran or you? You go to the people of knowledge before you make a conclusion, not after 15 years of making the conclusion.", "The Quran tells you it's clear, and there are people of knowledge, people of understanding, and there are lay people who don't necessarily grasp everything, and if something seems confusing to you, you go to the people of knowledge. Allah mentions People who are grounded in knowledge: you go ask them. You cannot say I went to ask someone of knowledge, because if you did, they've already have given you the answer that I have given you. If they were people of knowledge, you already would know the answer and they will give it to you. That means you ask the wrong people. So it's not that the Quran is to blame or Islam is to blame. It is you asking the people who are not the correct people.", "The people of knowledge that have experience with these issues, meaning they dealt with these issues before, have a long experience with dealing with all of these, you want to call it doubts or whatever. There's many people like this. You go and ask someone with knowledge like that, or a major scholar if you can reach them. Many major scholars, if you were to go any big part of the Islamic world, whether it is in the Gulf countries or any part of the Middle East or whatever, there's an Islamic scholar, they 