#passage(("I want to point out something very important: his main thing is polemics, and that is extremely important to keep in mind. I would always recommend for any [seeker] of knowledge: don't take your knowledge from someone who made the name from polemics, because people in polemics are, most the times, always on the defensive, and them being on the defensive so much causes them to reinterpret the deen. This is a reality. It's not only Muslim-Christian apologetics, but also even intersectarian debates has exact same issue. You'll find a Sunni who will water things down in Sunnism because of how much he clashes with Shias, and the same thing with Shias: you will notice [a Shia] weakening hadith like it's nobody's business. However, if he was to apply those standards against his hadith, he'd weaken everything.", "And that's what Shabir has fallen into. It seems like it started off with: \"Oh okay, this hadith, they're using this against Islam; well, it must be an unreliable hadith\", instead of accepting it for what it is. Instead of starting off with a background hadith and accepting these hadith because they're actually authentic according to our standards, he's rejecting everything that anyone is using against us, and that's a huge danger.", "So I always recommend people to avoid taking [ilm] from someone who's into apologetics, and that includes me. Don't take [ilm] from me. There's so many voices out there. This is how I started off, doing refutations in Sunni-Shiite matters and whatnot. I'm sure that I've been in some way affected; I hope not. There is a need to respond to allegations, shubahat and things like that, but you don't want to overdo it and go to an extreme where that's all that you're ever doing. It is important to definitely address certain issues, but at the same time, just get knowledge from the right people.", "There's so many resources; we have so many hundreds of years of scholarship that's so deep. If somebody comes across a hadith that they find to be problematic, we have scholars who have explained everything: \"You might have this issue, or you might perceive this thing\", and then they'll address it very clearly. Sometimes they say: \"This is how it is; if you don't like, it's your problem.\" And when that happens you have two choices. You either accept that it's your problem, and you accept that your values are not divine, and you accept that maybe you need to tweak some things within your head and you need to tweak your morals and values; or you have the other choice of rejecting the words and sayings and orders of the prophet because you thought you knew better.",), "Farid Responds, “Shocking Views of Shabir Ally”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uHU5W5UVHkQ", ((1408, "23:28–24:48"), (1489, "24:49–25:19"), (1519, "25:19–26:16"), (1578, "26:18–27:09"),), id: 3201)
