#passage(("Tafseer is a good starting point, for sure. If you read multiple or different tafseer, it will give you an explanation combining the verses. That is generally a good place to start. But if you don't, then you can always ask a person of knowledge, and they're always going to direct you.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Enquires About Qur'an Contradictions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0shFTfDDiro", ((1113, "18:33–18:51"),), id: 3212)
// U ML107:53-129 | The Muslim Lantern | 5
#passage(("The thing about us, as Muslims (unlike other traditions and Faiths and religions): we say that it's very important for people not to just jump into Scriptures on their own and dive deep, when it comes to the Hadith specifically, because we have tens of thousands of them. They have classifications, they have context, they need to be all brought in together in order for you to understand the context behind them. Some of these Hadiths are abrogated. There's many things that you can look at.", "So, for a lay person to just go open Bukhari and read a Narration: it could be talking about a story that is very long, that is mentioned in a few Narrations before or a few Narrations after, and he's just reading a part of it, because the Narrator can narrate a part of the incident. We can have an entire incident, and the Narrator can just mention one statement that was said. So the Prophet will say many things, and he said: \"And your God is One, and you should worship him\" within that long speech. And the Narrator will, for example, say: \"I heard the Prophet Muhammadﷺ say: \"Your God is One, you should worship him.\"\" Yes, the Prophet said that, and that makes perfect sense, but that's also within a wider context. So if you sometimes take some of these Narrations without the wider context, you can easily get confused. So for lay people, we will always say go to the people of knowledge when it comes to the Hadith.", "So, after reading the Quran, reading its exegesis, understanding the context of verses (because that's what exegeses do: the scholars bring the Hadiths and Narrations related to the verses of the Quran), now once that is done and you want to read the Hadith literature, go to the scholars, because there are Volumes explaining Bukhari, for example. There is a huge voluminous collection called فتح الباري (Fath alBari). Fath alBari is by a scholar called Ibn Hajar al'Asqalani, and he writes this voluminous volume, volumes upon volumes, of explanation of the Narrations in Sahih alBukhari, which are 6,000 Narrations. He brings the context, he gives you the explanation, all of that. He brings all the Narrations relevant to that, verses of the Quran relevant to that; he tells you if this specific Hadith is abrogated. Sahih Muslim, the other book other than Bukhari, has also an explanation by anNawawi, the Sharh (explanation) of Muslim; he explains Sahih Muslim in voluminous work again.", "So why do we say go to scholars? Because a layman doesn't have time to open volumes of different explanations. But if you go to that scholar that studied these books, they will save you the time. They will tell you the scholarly explanation of this Hadith and the Ahadith (Hadiths) related to it.", "And that is what makes Islam preserved also the way it is today. Because unlike, for instance (I'm going to use Christianity as an example), we don't have everyone just going by themselves to the books and reading, and coming up with their own understanding. That's why every Christian I talk to, whether it's about the Trinity, whether it's about Jesus, there's always different opinions. Anyone can open any two videos randomly from my channel: each Christian always has their own interpretation, understanding, view of the same topic, because people are reading and coming up with their own understanding. But we have a preserved Tradition, where these scholars will rely on the earliest generations, who are the Generation of the Prophet, who learned directly from the Prophet. And therefore our understanding will be the same, because it's taken from the Prophet who took Revelation.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Researcher Demands Evidence Against The Crucifixion Of Jesus! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IVUeaRBg02o", ((195, "3:15–3:42"), (225, "3:45–4:32"), (277, "4:37–5:40"), (346, "5:46–5:57"), (387, "6:27–7:19"),), id: 3213)
// U ML255:16-28 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
