#passage(("I'm sure you're thinking right now: \"But wait a second, all this stuff is in Arabic.\" Well, of course in Arabic. You think we're going to translate everything, make your life easy? You have to learn the language. You need to spend some time learning this beautiful language, so you can appreciate the Quran better. In all seriousness, I think it's just the Arab world hasn't really recognized the importance of getting some of this stuff out in other languages. And a lot of this stuff takes a lot of time, and of course translators want to get paid, so that's definitely another issue. Now, if you're going to get [Dala'il an-Nubuwwah], they have to translate it; it's going to take a few years. I think that you could learn the Arabic language before they're done with translating it. So that's my recommendation.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 29: Final”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uyXilafWwg4", ((159, "2:39–3:36"),), id: 3219)

=== 65.6 A daily habit of learning
// NODE P15.C02.S06
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR081:1-156 | Farid Responds | 7
#passage(("There's this major issue with the [ummah], and you have the ability to solve it, or play a role [in] solving it. And no, it's not donations; you're not going to have to spend any money. But I'll get to it after sharing this very brief story.", "Several years ago, I think it's maybe about 12 years ago, I was in a Masjid, and after the prayer ended the [sheikh] got up on the mic and he said we're going to have a competition, where there's going to be a specific book. It's going to be like 100 Pages, 150 pages, and it's going to be assigned, and there's going to be like three questions based on it. If you solve those three questions, you're going to receive some sort of a prize. I don't know how much; it was like $250 or something, first place. So what he was doing was he was announcing this in order to collect money for the first, second and third prize: whoever would like to donate for this cause, come to me after the end of prayers. So I went up to him; I was really happy, and I was like: that's a great idea, because it's something that's going to bring more people to learn about the [deen].", "After leaving the [masjid] I started telling people about this, and while I was telling people about this, the thought just occurred to me that it's a cash prize to learn about your religion. It's a cash prize to learn about your ideology, a cash prize to learn about your Creator and your relationship to your creator. That's really messed up. And then I realized how much I hate the idea: you're someone who actually adheres to a religion, but someone needs to give you money, a cash prize, for you to learn about it. So that's not the solution; I'm against the solution. No one should ever be offering some sort of a prize, some sort of a monetary gain, for others to learn their religion. And unfortunately the vast majority of people, not just Muslims, the vast majority of people in general, don't really care enough about the religion that they adhere to to actually learn about it, even though most people believe in an afterlife, a heaven and a hell. But they just don't care enough to learn about the religion.", "So with that in mind, how can you play a role in [solving] this? Well, if you are someone that actually cares about learning about their religion, and you are someone who actually is learning their religion, then share the information that you're learning. If you go to work with headphones and you're listening to lectures, speak to your co-workers about it, speak to your friends, speak to family members about it. If you're reading a book, tell people. When you're carrying that book, whether you're at home, outside, a friend's place, books in the car, whatever, tell people: I'm reading this, this is a really cool book, I'm learning about the Quran through reading about this book, I'm learning about the [Sirah] through this book. And that's how you get people interested, motivated.", "Everyone's got to start somewhere, and you may be the trigger to that. You may be the reason why someone begins to start caring about learning, because a lot of people simply love the [deen]. They just really appreciate the [deen], they feel that they're attached to the religion, but the idea of seeking knowledge is something that hasn't crossed their mind, and they need to see you doing it. So do that: go out of your way to tell people what you're currently reading or listening to or following, and hopefully that's going to play a role in them doing the same.", "And there was a time in our history (this is in like the [third] century, and he's in [Baghdad]): there were about like 40,000 people at his [majlis], at his Hadith Gathering. Can you imagine that? 40,000; that like fills up, I think, Chelsea's football stadium. So the masses were into learning about the religion. The masses: I'm not speaking about students of knowledge, I'm not speaking about Scholars; the masses, the lay person, was attending Hadith classes. Can you imagine that? Of course, that's in Baghdad; it's biggest city in the world at the time, the center of the Muslim world, if not the world in general. But it's still a huge number, 40,000. That's not something that happens today in the [majlis] of Hadith, despite the population of any religion significantly increasing. The numbers aren't there; it's because people, even though they are attached to their religion, they simply don't care enough about their religion to actually spend time learning about it.", "So that's my solution for the [ummah]. If you're not someone who's studying, if you're not someone who's taking seeking knowledge to that next level, if you're someone who at the moment is entry level or Basics level, then take it up [a notch], spend more time learning. You don't have to dedicate yourself to this: spending 15 minutes a day reading a book, reading about a field that you're interested in. You don't have to specialize, but read and learn about something that you are interested in, and hopefully with time it's not just going to be a change within you; inshallah it will be a change within your community, those around you.",), "Farid Responds, “A Major Problem in the Muslim Ummah... and a Solution”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JW2KUzAPVi8", ((5, "0:05–0:24"), (28, "0:28–1:47"), (116, "1:56–3:24"), (204, "3:24–4:20"), (260, "4:20–5:01"), (304, "5:04–6:21"), (381, "6:21–7:08"),), id: 3220)
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML360:1527-1600 | The Muslim Lantern | 5
#passage(("I've got a specific time of the day, or, let's not just say one, because it depends on what I'm doing. There's a specific time for Quran, and there's a specific time for reading a specific book, and then there is a specific time for general research on all of these different topics: Atheism, science and cosmology, all of these issues. Point is, if you have a system, you stick to the system. For example: if after Fajr I'm going to do my Quran revision, which includes Tafseer (Quran explanation), if I'm doing it for 30-40 minutes, then every day I'm doing it for 30-40 minutes.", "The point is, it's not that you don't have time; it is that you're wasting your time on the wrong things. Just open your phone, click screen time: screen time is an answer to how much rubbish you're reading on a daily basis, or probably listening to things which are not important, especially if it's in this TikTok, this nonsense. So point is this: just make a system for yourself, 1:30 hour a day. I doubt that no one doesn't have 1:30 hour a day. Keep it after prayers, so you remember, you don't lose the time. Let's say half an hour after Fajr, half an hour after Asr and half an hour after Isha, but stick to them! Don't say: I've got this thing to do, therefore I'm going to ignore my 30 minutes of today and I'm going to do this thing. This is where we got our issues: is the commitment.", "30 minutes will go through so much. You will grow so much! You will finish books upon books, and the more you read, the more quicker you read. You'll finish a complete amount of research; if you memorise the Quran, you'll be memorising the whole Quran if you stick to that. This is my method; this is what my teachers have taught me: just have a specific time of the day. It's all about commitment; if you commit to it, you will get the benefit.", "The problem, what some people do, is that they want (this is what this age of Technology have taught us) maximum amount of knowledge in the shortest amount of time. This is what people started to have in their mind: I want in two hours to now I've understand everything; I want to read this book and now I'm able to do Dawah (Invitation to Islam). Some people ask me: which book do you recommend so I can do Da'wah? It's not that you open the door, you close the door; it's not like this. And that's what our teachers [taught] us: it takes time. Do not look at the Mountain. Mountain is a Mountain, but if you start taking one Rock by one Rock, the Mountain is gone before you know it. Don't look at the Mountain and try to move the full Mountain. This is what people do: they want to get so much knowledge in short amounts of time, and when they don't, they give up!", "If you're consistent and you spend a small amount of time, like the Prophetﷺ said: \"The most beloved deeds to Allah are small but they're consistent\", even if they're small, but they're consistent. Consistency is key. If you're doing the consistency, you will get the knowledge. It doesn't matter how busy you are; you've got 1:30 hour in your day to do what I'm telling you.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslims Scientifically And Rationally Dismantle Evolution! Muhammed Ali & @SubboorAhmadAbbasi”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3TrwJOx-kUM", ((5863, "1:37:43–1:38:25"), (5916, "1:38:36–1:39:20"), (5964, "1:39:24–1:39:45"), (5990, "1:39:50–1:40:32"), (6035, "1:40:35–1:40:49"),), id: 3221)

=== 65.7 Reading books and building a library
// NODE P15.C02.S07
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR092:1-147 | Farid Responds | 7
#passage(("There is a major issue with our society and with how the contemporary Muslim is in terms of acquiring information: today most people will watch a video but will not read the book. For those of you that are wondering what's the easiest way, or what's the quickest way, of becoming knowledgeable: it's simply by reading, of course, and returning to Scholars or students of knowledge when you have questions that have to do with those readings.", "Now, the first question that comes to mind is how to read. Well, we all know how to read, but it really has to do with what to read, and, when you're reading it, how far to go. I'm someone who strongly believes in casually reading, not forcing yourself to read. Don't read with the intention of finishing the book. I'm saying read; I don't mean read Twitter posts, I don't mean read articles, I mean read books. Books are written with the author's intention to give you the most important and hardest to come across information, and they've presented to you, sometimes free of charge, sometimes at a really low price. They've saved you weeks of research, months of research, and all you got to do is consume it. So why not?", "When you read, you get bored after half an hour, you take a break. Don't force yourself. When you force yourself, you start becoming lazy in the way you acquire the information. Your eyes go over the words on the page, but the information doesn't stick to your mind. You will often think: what did I just read on this page? Doesn't seem like I remember much of it, even though I just finished reading it. So you should read with a fresh mind. If you get bored, go somewhere else, do something fun, talk to a friend, chill with a family member, but of course for a short period of time, half an hour, 20 minutes, whatever; work out for a bit at home, do some push-ups, and then go back to your reading after taking that short break.", "Another thing to keep in mind is: buy multiple books. Buy multiple books on the subject, buy multiple books [you're] interested in. Don't just buy one book and think to yourself: yeah, I'm going to buy a second book after I'm done reading this one. It's not something I'd recommend. The more you buy, the better. Sure, you might feel guilty: oh, I didn't finish reading this book and I'm going to buy a second one. Yeah, feel guilty, and then push yourself to read a bit more. I haven't even read like a tenth of what I have, but it pushes me to read more. And there have been so many occasions in which I think to myself: oh, I wish I had something on this specific subject; and, oh, there it is, right there, there's a copy of a book on that specific subject. I didn't even know that I had that; something I bought 20 years ago or something.", "So buy books. And when you buy a book, the chances of you reading it is a lot higher than if you just Download a pdf, because you don't feel it's worth. But when you have the book, when you bought it, when it's in your hand, you're flipping the pages, it's something that you've invested in. Even if it's a small amount of money, it means more than 2, three megabytes, 10 megabytes that are stuck on your laptop.", "Another important aspect is to read what interests you. If you're interested in a specific field, you read in that specific field. Don't read about something that you have absolutely no interest in, especially when you're starting out, because you're going to get bored extremely fast and you're just going to drop it. And it shouldn't be something that you're forcing yourself to do. If you're forcing yourself to do it, chances are you're not going to be benefiting too much; but if it's something that you have a passion for, then inshallah you're going to excel.", "Another point is: you take notes. Fill your books with notes. You can have them in the front inner cover; put notes in there. Anything you come across that seems strange, that is information that you'd like to highlight, put a note, and put a page number, so that one day, maybe in the future, maybe not now, maybe 5, 10 years from now, you're going to feel like: oh, this is some really interesting stuff that I came across a long time ago, but where is it? And the notes will help you find it really fast. I would return to a book like over 10 times, and I'm like: if only I took that note, if only I put a page number. And of course I eventually did, after I started taking notes properly. I spent many years not taking notes, unfortunately.",), "Farid Responds, “How to become Knowledgeable”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eeQfEsId39M", ((23, "0:23–1:23"), (83, "1:23–2:42"), (162, "2:42–3:43"), (223, "3:43–4:35"), (277, "4:37–5:04"), (309, "5:09–5:50"), (352, "5:52–6:58"),), id: 3222)
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
