#passage(("The righteous, in general, they say: if you come to war, would you come without a weapon? Let's say now we're going to fight: you going to come without a weapon, or how are you going to come? You're going to come prepared. If you come to a setting of knowledge, whatever setting of knowledge it is, and you don't come with a pen and paper, then you don't have your weapons with you. Unless, [mashallah], we're sitting here with [Bukhari]; if we are sitting with [Bukhari], okay, it's okay, you don't need a pen [and] paper, you're going to recollect all the information after we're done. Unless this is the case, the paper will help you, because we're also trying to organize the knowledge for you by giving you points, which would help you to recall the information when you want to revise them. And in general, we don't mean these ones only, but any setting of knowledge: should be prepared.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Aqeedah (Creed) And Its Significance In Islam | AIM Conference 2024 NJ | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ULnwXbVf_cM", ((3322, "55:22–56:16"),), id: 3223)
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR092:148-248 | Farid Responds | 4
#passage(("Another motivation, or another thing that will make you knowledgeable, is if you read for others, not just for yourself. If you read and you obtain information and you keep it to yourself, it's most likely going to wither away in a short while. But if you read with the intention of helping others, whether you want to teach your family, you want to teach your people around you, you want to correct people's misconceptions about specific topics, you want to play a role in [dawah] to other people and bringing them to Islam (this is a noble thing, of course), reading [with that] intention, it's going to benefit you more than if you're reading just to keep that information to yourself. And from the [barakah] of Knowledge, from the best blessings of knowledge, is sharing in it with others. And this, by the way, is scientifically proven; it's not just me being extra spiritual here or anything like that. Because if you read something and you write it, it sticks to your mind; but if you teach it, if you tell someone: by the way, I learned this, this, this, that's the best way to retain that information. It's by teaching.", "Now, keep in mind that doing this reading will change you as a person, in the way you access knowledge, in the way you deal with knowledge. Once Upon a Time, you're someone that accepts everything: everything you see, everything that's written on a piece of paper, everything that's written on a page, everything that's written on a tweet, will have equal weight to the opposing point of view, if you're a blank slate. But with reading, you become someone who's technical about information, you become someone that's able to scrutinize, you become critical. So it changes you as a person. You today, if you haven't started reading yet, is going to be a completely different person than that person who has spent years reading. And especially if you're someone who reads in one specific field, it changes the way your mind works. So that's something to look forward to.", "I know it sounds strange, but I look at who I was when I first started reading, when I was like 21: I was a completely different person. I used to have the craziest beliefs, I'd have the most unjustifiable ideas, just ideas that sounded good to me. And now today, Subhan Allah, ideas, they sound good to me, but I reject them due to the lack of proof, because of who I am today and how I've changed due to reading.", "A very quick call to action: right now, today, if you're not someone who has been reading, who's taking Reading seriously, right now think about a field that you're interested in, find a book that you're interested in, order that book today. Doesn't have to be something expensive; don't spend $100. Buy something that's equal to the price of a dinner meal or a lunch meal, and buy that, order it, get it shipped to you, and read it the moment that you get it. If you're in a noisy Place, find a place that's quiet for you to read; you don't want too many distractions around you.",), "Farid Responds, “How to become Knowledgeable”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eeQfEsId39M", ((421, "7:01–8:23"), (507, "8:27–9:33"), (573, "9:33–10:09"), (609, "10:09–10:59"),), id: 3224)
// U FR050:1-51 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("The short version for the story is: I was seeing a lot of [shubuhat]. This is going to be about around maybe 18 years ago. I was someone who's active on Islamic discussion forums, and I'm seeing [shubuhat] everywhere, and I'm seeing all these sources being quoted. I'm not familiar with these sources; some of them I am, [Bukhari], Muslim and [whatnot]. I didn't have a copy of [Bukhari] or Muslim or anything like that. I don't think I even had a single book with me at the time; well, maybe a couple, nothing serious. But I remember it was around that time where I thought to myself: I need to start learning about these sources. I need to start buying books. And I made myself a little list of books that I was interested in purchasing, and I went to the mall. This was during the time in which I didn't even know of Islamic bookstores. I probably never been to an Islamic bookstore in my life, but I went to the mall, and had an Islamic section, and one of those books that was on the list was actually available for sale.", "What they had wasn't a 20 volume version; it was a condensed version, in three volumes. And this is one of the first things that I ever bought. And it's a wonderful book for biographies, for biographical information about scholars of hadith, about kings, about warriors, about poets. It's not something that I went through from cover to cover, of course, but it's a great reference book. It's something that changed my life, because it was that one first step. It was quite a large step actually. Usually you'd pick up something small, but I went for something that was quite large immediately. And with time, because it's quite large, buying other texts became easier. My copy of [Bukhari] is in one volume, my copy of [Muslim is] in one volume, and I started buying other works like [Bukhari] and Muslim, perhaps two years after I bought.",), "Farid Responds, “incomplete motivational video about books”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9PCjn9opouc", ((21, "0:21–1:57"), (143, "2:23–4:42"),), id: 3225)

