#passage(("Life is short, and the vast majority of us, we're just going to read probably one [Sirah] book in our whole lives, maybe two; for most of us maybe even none. But if you're going to be reading one [Sirah] book, I'd like you to read the best [Sirah] book that is accessible to you. Now keep in mind, I'm going to be talking about [best Sirah] book and not best lecture series, and the reason: well, two main reasons. One is, with books, they're heavily referenced, which is something that's extremely important; they're a lot more accurate than video lectures. And two, video lectures are quite lengthy; there are series out there that are like over 100 hours long, and if you're someone that's interested in getting the information in a tenth of the time, then perhaps a book is what suits you.", "I asked on Twitter about the top recommendations for a [Sirah] book, and the vast majority suggested the Sealed Nectar, and in second place the [Sirah] by [Martin Lings]. I realize not too long after asking that question that the vast majority of those have answered have either read one [Sirah] book or none. I even asked my mom, who recommended the Sealed Nectar to me like 20 years ago, why she even recommended the Sealed Nectar: was it due to some sort of a comparison? No, it was simply that she heard it was the best [Sirah] book out there, and therefore she recommended it. So the vast majority of people that recommend books on [Sirah] haven't compared them to anything else. So naturally what I did is I went through those books that were recommended, and I started to compare them one to another, and finally I came to a conclusion as to what I believe the best book of [Sirah] is.", "Now, the main issue with the vast majority of what's written on the topic of [Sirah] is that they're either too short (and if you want to learn about the prophet, peace be upon him, in his life, you would be interested in the details); the other issue is a lot of them don't provide proper referencing, footnotes, and a lot of them don't provide a variety when it comes to the works that they're quoting.", "Now, in regards to the Sealed Nectar: the reason why it's easily the most popular book, at least in the English language, is because the author of the Sealed Nectar won an award in the 70s, I believe. The book won an award in 1976, and due to that, for the longest time, it's been seen as the best book of [Sirah]. But it has been 50 years, and you'd expect something in the past 50 years to surpass it.", "Technically, the best book of [Sirah] is a traditional source, and it's simply due to the amount of details in that book. However, I would only recommend for those that know the Arabic language, and for those that have the ability to study the chains of narrations of each and every single report, which is not the simplest of tasks. So I wouldn't recommend it to the average reader. But what I would recommend is [Mahdi Rizqullah's] \"A Biography of the [Prophet of] Islam\", for several reasons. It's in two volumes, around 900 pages, and it [ticks] all the right boxes. It's heavily referenced in the footnotes, and of course that's where this book shines, because the author relies on a vast number of sources, a huge variety, masha allah. It's not incredibly long like [Sallabi's] book, which is three volumes long, and I think that's just way too much. And what I really like about this book is that he relies heavily on modern contemporary works that have studied the [Sirah] as well. So he was able to make use of the Arabic works, but now you have it in English form. Also, the book isn't too expensive. I've seen high prices on Amazon, but if you have an English bookstore around you, and they supply [Darussalam] books, then you should be able to pick this up for quite a cheap price. I picked mine up for something like 20, maybe even a bit less.", "Just to give you a bit of a taste, when we compare the Sealed Nectar and [Mahdi Rizqullah's] book: you have an issue with referencing with the Sealed Nectar. Here's an example. On page 106, it's talking about the persecution that the Muslims face in Mecca, and it says here: \"The uncle of Uthman used to wrap Uthman up in a mat of palm leaves and set fire under him.\" When you go into the footnote, it says volume 1, page 57, and that's it. It just provides you with a reference right there, and doesn't go into any detail. On the other hand, in [Mahdi Rizqullah's] book, it says: \"Uncle would wrap him up in a mat made from palm leaves and then smoke him\" (which is a bit of a clunky translation). Anyway, in the footnotes, the same source, volume 1, page 52, he adds: \"However, we could not trace out [Mansurpuri's] source.\" He saves you the time. So [Mahdi Rizqullah] saved me the time of going back to [Mansurpuri's] work to see what his source was, by simply saying that [Mansurpuri] did not provide a source. What you'll find a lot of is [Mahdi Rizqullah] providing the gradings of pretty much every single thing that he's quoting.", "So those are the pros. And the cons aren't too many, but, for example, sometimes he will include inauthentic material due to his reliance on other scholars that have assumed that some reports are authentic. But that happens with the vast majority of authors; it's something that is quite hard to escape. And unfortunately the translation in some sections is a bit clunky. However, those things don't take away from this very important book, and hands down it is the best book in [Sirah] for the English-speaking audience. If you haven't read the book, I strongly recommend it.",), "Farid Responds, “What is the Best Book of Seerah in English?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4PGwYQdj0Gk", ((13, "0:13–1:11"), (73, "1:13–2:25"), (145, "2:25–2:54"), (176, "2:56–4:05"), (247, "4:07–6:04"), (364, "6:04–7:47"), (468, "7:48–8:34"),), id: 3226)
