#passage(("No Muslim in the world would ever be affected by such silly arguments. The only person that may be affected is someone with the absolute worst opinion of Islam, someone that sees Islam as beyond just barbaric and sexually driven, someone that believes that the Quran is a book of sex and that seventh-century Arabs only converted to Islam because the Quran was filled with sexual fantasies on every page. This could not be further away from the truth. Most Muslims, yes, even the ones that read Arabic, know what the Quran is saying, and these are civilized people [that] happen to live in the modern world. There's not a single Muslim in my society that is part of a terrorist organization. I personally don't know [of] any rapists either. Most Muslims I know that are married only have one wife. Many of them, myself included, are educated in the English language and consume media that is produced in the West. Many of them watch documentaries because they're educational and interesting. Many of them enjoy great works like Citizen Kane, The Godfather and The Good, the Bad and the Ugly. Many Muslims are god-fearing individuals that will smile in your face, give to charity and help those that need it. And just like Christians, there are some bad apples: bad apples that sell drugs, bad apples that knowingly bring harm to others, and bad apples with terrible taste and enjoy garbage like the Harry Potter series or Naruto.", "So what am I trying to say? You may disagree with Islam and you may believe that Islam is false, however that doesn't mean that we are evil people that believe that we were born to have orgasms. Most of us, believe it or not, believe that Islam is true, and we have our reasons for believing so. We do not believe that lying is a good thing, we believe that lying is bad. And instead of taking that knowledge from Christian Prince, someone who we should all agree by now is a liar and very ill-mannered, perhaps it should be best to ask a knowledgeable Muslim next time you're at the supermarket or [at a] community fair or at the hair salon: why are you a Muslim? And maybe, just maybe, you can have a conversation that is more respectful, fair and loving than you expected.", "A neighbor of mine recently attempted to invite me to studying their religion. It brought me great joy, because I felt that they were reaching out to me and sharing what they perceived to be true with me. I did not perceive my neighbor as evil. Instead I thanked them, met with their community, sat down and had lengthy respectful discussions about their faith and mine. Our time is very limited, and we shouldn't be wasting what little time we have on bigots that are trying to make a living by trash talking and degrading 1/5 of the world's population. Now, this is not an invitation to accept Islam, by the way; this is an invitation to not hate Islam, because, believe you me, we could do with a lot less hatred in this world.",), "Farid Responds, “Christian Prince Challenged Me to Refute This!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DWbut8m7nBk", ((363, "6:03–7:47"), (467, "7:47–8:53"), (533, "8:53–9:54"),), id: 3285)

