#passage(("What I appreciate the most is honesty, and I think what God appreciates the most in these kinds of discussions is to be sincere and honest. So, we don't do these things for people. We don't do these things to try to prove what we believe in.", "So, if you come and tell me you studied Islam for 10 to 12 years, that's a big claim to make, because your knowledge would be more than the average Muslim. There's no doubt about that. Why? Because the average Muslim does not sit down to study Islam for 10 to 12 years. He goes through life, learning about Islam. There's a difference between the two things. If I make a goal of sitting down to study something for 10 to 12 years, I would know more than the average person who's just living his life, and throughout his life, he's picking up information about this and that, which is what the average Muslim does, and the average Christian as well: you just live your life and you hear information around you and that’s it. So, if I sit down to study the Bible for 10 to 12, without a shadow of a doubt, I know more than the average Christian, because I have actually looked at: okay, what proves this religion, contradictions, all of that. So, to say to me, you've studied Islam for 10 to 12 years – I'll tell you the truth. I don't think anyone would study Islam for 10 to 12 years and doesn't become a Muslim, except if he is someone who's not sincere with himself. That's the only condition that I would put. I’m not saying this in a rude way, but I will test your knowledge, because you're saying you were, for 10 to 12 years, studying Islam. So, I‘ll ask you basic questions and see.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Claims He Studied Islam For 12 Years?! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gvPZJMH0co4", ((223, "3:43–3:51"), (237, "3:57–5:10"),), id: 3291)
