=== 66.15 Aggressive dawah and insulting language
// NODE P15.C03.S15
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML037:16-295 | The Muslim Lantern | 16
#passage(("I do sometimes videos like this, where I see there's a big issue that is happening. The purpose is not to correct specific individuals or attack specific individuals. It's about the issue itself. It's about addressing the issue at hand. It's about advising the general public, people becoming aware of the issue that is taking place. And the individuals, if anybody is doing that wrong thing that I'm about to talk about, they would know. I don't need to mention a person by name. And I guarantee you, when I'm talking about this issue, a lot of people will start having these names pop up in their mind. And I would say you are a part of the problem, because you are following people who are doing these issues and you're not correcting them. You're not advising them. In fact, as we will see, some people are even justifying these issues that are happening.", "So the point is to address the topic, address the idea, for Muslims to become aware, to advise the individuals who are doing this in general, and to advise the Muslims that are supporting them, justifying what they do and thinking that this is okay. And at the same time clarifying to the non-Muslims that if there's a non-Muslim who thinks that this is what Islam teaches, no, this is not what Islam teaches. This is our responsibility. And whether people listen or not, in the end people will do what they want to do. But I think it's very crucial and important to address this issue.", "So what's the issue? The issue is that we have today a lot of young duat, brothers and even recently sisters. They're doing what they would call [da'wah]. But the purpose of what they refer to as da'wah is to essentially defeat the other person, humiliate them, dunk on them, make them look like an idiot. This is the purpose. Now, I'm not talking about once in a while, for example, holding someone accountable and pressing them on a point, being assertive with them. I'm not talking about that. You have to be balanced. At some times, you have to be assertive. I'm talking about the overwhelming majority of the content that is being done. The purpose behind it is: I destroyed this person. I completely annihilated this person. I finished this person.", "You have 90% of the content about this idea, and then in the discussion, when the discussion takes place, you see people using thug-like language. What do I mean by that? Slurs, insulting people, referring to their body parts, referring to humiliating insults about them and things that they do and stuff like that. I've seen some people go to extreme extents with insults, and I've seen some people [inappropriately] addressing women about things which are intimate in nature, and they're making jokes about it. How is this [da'wah]? Someone explain this to me. How is this Islamic behavior? Now the sisters: there's a lot more issues to talk about, like sisters inappropriately laughing, addressing men in a shameless manner. There's a huge issue to talk about.", "So we need to understand we're representing this religion, representing the [ummah] as a whole, not just representing ourselves, and we need to keep that in mind. And the people watching us, there's Muslims and there's non-Muslims, and there's people who just look for any reason and excuse to essentially say, look, Muslims are bad. As a Muslim, as [duat], we need to uphold the highest levels of mannerisms. This is how the prophet was. This is how the people who were doing [da'wah], which are the prophets and the messengers, were. This is the characteristic and behavior that we need to take. Otherwise, do not do dawa; go do something else. If you have issues with mannerisms, if you have issues with addressing people, this is not something for you. Nobody's asking you to be perfect, but this is not for you. You're going to do more harm than good by doing this.", "The even bigger issue is that we have individuals justifying this behavior. I think this is bigger issue than the issue itself happening: you have Muslims saying, \"Oh, it's okay. No problem. They're young, so it's okay. They can do it. They're Muslims, so we going to defend them even if they're doing wrong.\" No. Prophet said, aid your brother if he's oppressor or he's oppressed. So the [companions] said, if he's oppressed, yes, we'll aid him, but if he's oppressor, how? You stop him from his oppression. We stop the person from his oppression. We don't justify just because we like someone, or someone is a Muslim, or they're doing dawa. And this behavior is not only new young duat; it's been existing in old duat as well. This needs to be addressed, whether it's the new duat or the old duat. I'm talking about the issue itself, the problem.", "People will justify this behavior. For example, you have some people say, \"Oh, they're very knowledgeable,\" and they're talking about, for example, the knowledge in Christianity, which I can see in the overwhelming majority, and the people in Tik Tok. It's about knowledge in Christianity; they research a few things, learn a few things Islamically. Is this what we consider knowledge? When you look at these people, early 20s or maybe younger, or even the teens: a lot of people who are watching themselves are not knowledgeable; they're ignorant, so they see anyone, they think this person is knowledgeable. Knowledge takes time, and most importantly wisdom takes time. When you're young, when you're [hot-blooded], don't get in the forefront unless you've spent time with the scholars, the people of knowledge. You've learned mannerisms. You learn mannerisms before you learn the knowledge. [Imam Malik], he would talk about how his mother would teach him: go and learn manners before you learn the knowledge. So people are separating these two things. No, they're part of the religion. The [Prophet], he said, I was only sent to perfect manners. He was sent as a mercy for mankind.", "So we cannot justify this behavior that the people are doing. It's completely unexcusable. These Muslims are trying to justify this cursing, insulting, belittling others. [Prophet] said: the believer, he's not the one who curses others, insults them, attacks their [honour] or has a filthy language. This is not the believer. And the prophet is talking about the believer, let alone a [da'i], a caller to Islam, who is supposed to be on a higher standard than the average believer. You're inviting people to Islam. People are looking at you thinking, \"Oh, this is the representative of Islam.\"", "Then you have people justifying this behavior. They say, \"We need to give them back the same energy.\" Are we Muslims? We have our own manners, manners Allah and the Quran taught us. Allah says in the Quran: repel that which is evil with that which is better. Allah doesn't tell us to do the same thing. Now some people say they insult Allah and the messenger. Yeah, they do insult Allah and the messenger. What does Allah tell you to do when you engage with people who insult Allah and the messenger? Allah told us in the Quran, in two verses, he says: if you hear those who are insulting, mocking Allah and the messenger, do not sit with them, leave their place. Allah tells us in two places. Allah repeatedly is telling you. So don't tell me you give them back the same energy. Allah told you: don't be engaging with these people.", "Problem is, for purposes of gaining followers, you'd have a lot of these young Muslims go and talk to the [Islamophobes]. You're going to them and you're bringing Muslims come and watch you, because you're a Muslim. So they go watch. It's like a football match: who's going to score points. And the debate ends; nobody changes anything in most cases. Muslims follow the Muslim, Christians follow the Christian, in most cases. Of course there are some benefits that can come out in some debates if it's done the right way. You go to them yourself and then you complain that they insult Allah and the messenger, and then you justify that and say, \"I'm going to insult them back because they're insulting Allah and the messenger.\" That's not how it works. That's not what Allah told us in the Quran. So do not pick and choose verses. Allah told us in the Quran: do not insult the gods of those people who worship other than Allah, because they would insult Allah. You're not supposed to be engaging with these people. You're not supposed to be going and talking to them and then behaving the same way that they do. There is a difference between us and them.", "Then the people will justify this behavior. How they will justify this behavior? They will go to verses of the Quran where Allah, for example, say that the believers are harsh or firm with the disbelievers. Harsh and firm means assertive when need be. Who do we have as an example? Allah told us in the Quran: you have in the prophet Muhammad the best of examples. He's the example for us to look at. Was he cursing and insulting and [slurring]? He wasn't. The people who say this, they know the [Prophet] was like this, and they know that the default in the dawa, you have to be showing the best manners. Allah says: do not argue with the people of the book except with that which is best, except those who transgress. This is referring to fighting in war, the people who are going to fight; so they transgress, you'll stop the transgression. It's not talking about cursing and insulting.", "Some people will go to the [Sirah] and bring an example of Abu Bakr: he said this about a goddess of the disbelievers, [al-Lat]. We're talking about in the midst of war between believers and disbelievers, and the companions are being insulted, and the prophet is being insulted, that the believers are going to run away; and about a certain type of disbelievers, muharib, someone who's an enemy of Islam fighting you in war, not an average disbeliever who can be a [mu'ahad], someone you have a treaty with, or just a normal disbeliever. We're talking about in war, [muharib], who you're going to fight against, who were insulting Allah and the messenger. And it's from [Abu Bakr]. It's not from the prophet. Allah told us to take prophet Muhammad as an example. And it's one example in the entire [Sirah]. They go and stick to that, void of all of this context: he did this, so I can do this. And it's not that I can do this as an exception one time in my life, like Abu Bakr [Siddiq] did. No, I'm going to do this every day, and I'm going to use a justification: one incident that happened with [Abu Bakr] one time in his life. It's a joke, honestly, and that's why I'm saying it needs to be called out, and the people who justify this are a part of the problem.", "You know that they have the filthiest of mouth. But that is their characteristic. Prophet said that's not the believer. It's not the characteristic of the believer. You cannot behave like them and use it as an excuse. I made a post on Twitter addressing this, and Islamophobes took this as an opportunity: \"Oh, look, he's right,\" because they have no real arguments against Islam. So now they're trying to clutch to anything. But that doesn't matter, because Allah said that we are the best nation for one reason: we [enjoin] the good and forbid the evil. As the hadith says, the religion is [sincere advice], and we are the best nation because we advise one another. If someone is doing wrong, we will say they're doing wrong. The non-Muslims will see that we call out that bad behavior that happens within our ranks. Now when you look at them, they support each other. They push each other further. When they show this behavior, they justify it, make excuses for it. We cannot be like this. We call out this behavior clearly. We say this is wrong.", "I want to make something very clear. I'm more than happy that a lot of youngsters are trying to get involved in the dawa, invite people to Islam. Wallah, I'm happy for it. And this is the future that we need. We need people who carry the torch. We need new Muslims who are engaged in dawa, but in the right way, in the right manner. As Allah says, say, oh prophet, say this is my way, I call to Allah. The way of the messenger is the way we need to carry. This is the next generation; we need to advise them. When I started doing dawah, I made [mistakes] here and there; the senior brothers would give me advice, I would listen to them. It's important, it's needed.", "What is the dangers of this trend? The first thing is that it is changing the perception of what [da'wah] is in the minds of the people. People now don't understand that [da'wah] means an invitation where you call to the way of your lord in good mannerisms and good speech. Purpose is not to prove yourself right. Purpose is not to debunk the other and humiliate him and make him look like an idiot. Is to call the people to Allah, not to ourselves being winners. So changing the perception of [da'wah] to a competition, like we are in the coliseum and this guy's winning: oh, let's cheer for that person. No. So that's the biggest danger. And the second thing is it's pushing people away from Islam, some non-Muslims, and ruining our image. We need to present these superior morals; we need to stick to our own morals that our religion teaches. And the last thing is the influencing the other youth. Because some youth are doing it now, it's influencing the other youth to do the exact same thing, to go on these platforms and Tik Tok, and then they start cursing and insulting. I saw this person do it; oh, if he's doing it, he has a YouTube channel, then I must be able to do it too. It must be okay.", "So this trend needs to stop. It's a dangerous trend that needs to be called out, needs to be stopped. What do we need to do as Muslims? We need to call this out, warn the people. If the people persist in their behavior, then we need to boycott them. We need to show clearly, as Muslims, this is not in line with the teachings of Islam. I'm not perfect. I do sometimes in my dawa make a mistake, not show the best behavior. I'm a human being. It happens. However, we're not talking about the exceptions. We're talking about the general rule. Now if someone, in most situations, he is not good in his behavior, he curses, he insults, then [da'wah] is not for you.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “This Trend NEEDS To Stop Now!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mc-2HiePa94", ((62, "1:02–2:02"), (122, "2:02–2:49"), (169, "2:49–3:38"), (221, "3:41–4:52"), (304, "5:04–5:55"), (355, "5:55–6:45"), (405, "6:45–8:04"), (484, "8:04–8:44"), (524, "8:44–9:50"), (593, "9:53–10:55"), (655, "10:55–11:39"), (699, "11:39–12:59"), (792, "13:12–14:13"), (853, "14:13–14:54"), (901, "15:01–16:14"), (974, "16:14–16:56"),), id: 3339)
