#passage(("Muslim, I understand that when you come across clips like this, it angers you and you want to respond in a [viler] way than they acted towards the prophet, peace be upon him. But you need to be aware of one really important thing. You need to remember that there needs to be a difference between you and them, because you have truth on your side and all they have is falsehood. So that truth needs to affect you in a way. And if you resort to what they resort to, then there's no difference between you and them. You're not better than they are. So if they make claims against Islam that are built upon lies, you need to respond to them with claims about Christianity that are facts built on truth. If they distort your report in a sexual way, then you respond with facts and figures and contradictions and inconsistencies in a respectful way.", "A lot of Christians that you do come across online do have sealed hearts. However, there is a large percentage of them that are open to hearing what you have to say. And all they need is for someone to say, \"Hey, look, this is a contradiction. These are statements from the early church fathers that conflict with the trinity. This is proof that the disciples did not die due to their belief in the resurrection of Jesus.\" That's all you got to do. And whatever you do, don't sink to their level.", "When you do things like that, you are sinking to their level. And when you do that, they respond by twisting another hadith sexually. And then you respond by twisting another biblical verse sexually. And it goes on, so on and so forth. And in the end, even that Christian who has his heart open, who is willing to listen to the opposing point of view, when he sees you acting in that way, that person's heart will be sealed as well. And that David Wood that you hate so much is the person that you've become.",), "Farid Responds, “The Face of Christian Apologetics”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JQB6KcegfBM", ((281, "4:41–5:29"), (332, "5:32–6:05"), (404, "6:44–7:20"),), id: 3341)

=== 66.16 Debating anti-Islam critics: value and harms
// NODE P15.C03.S16
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML018:1-128 | The Muslim Lantern | 7
#passage(("We have a serious issue that needs to be addressed, but before I mention what the issue is, I want to say that the majority of the people watching are responsible for this issue. Why? Because the things that we do as Muslims, what you click on, what you view, what you choose to watch and engage with, has a huge influence on what other people see, has a huge influence on what other people believe. You may think you can click on something when you're on social media, it doesn't have an influence. It has a massive influence, not only on the creators, but it has a massive influence on the algorithm, what other people are going to see in the end. So we need to understand as Muslims, we are responsible; we need to be careful about what we click on, because we can be responsible on the day of judgment. You could be sinning by what you're clicking on, what you're choosing to engage with and promote to other people.", "So what's this issue? The issue is we have a group of Muslims that keep debating the same Islam haters, Islamophobes, enemies of Islam, whatever name you want to give them, repeatedly, over and over again, on the same topic, on the same idea. I'm not talking about generally the idea of debating. I'm talking about debating specific vile individuals that are insulting the messenger of Allah, are evil enemies of Islam, that the only thing they do is not promote their own religion; it's to attack Islam 24/7. That is the main purpose behind their existence. So I'm talking about this issue of Muslims not only debating them, the same people, but also debating them on the same thing, over and over again.", "I briefly talked about this in the last live stream, and what happened is someone clipped that part and uploaded it on Twitter. And then all of these Islam haters, without exception, all the popular ones, all of them have commented on this. Do you know why they comment on this? Because I'm talking about their livelihood. They understand if Muslims with platforms stop engaging with these people, stop debating them, nobody will care about it. Nobody will look at them. This idea of speakers corners: there was many people in speakers corner that were well-known. Why were they well-known? Because Muslims engaged with them. So Muslims made them popular. But when Muslims stopped engaging with them, nobody hears about them anymore. Nobody cares about them anymore. Nobody watches them anymore. They have nothing of substance to offer to their audiences. The only reason these people are popular is because the Muslims are making them popular. You don't believe me, go on their channels. Look at the view count in their channels when they're doing their own thing, versus when a Muslim with a platform is engaging with or debating with them. You will see the views are skyrocketing. Why? Because people are more interested when you see, \"Oh, two groups of people are engaging with each other, colliding with each other.\"", "And what do they say when they comment on the clip, those Islam haters? They have to, of course, make an excuse. They say, \"Oh, look, Muslims are losing. That's why they don't engage with us.\" These people live in a delusion. They keep saying nonsense like Islam is dying. You go to the stats: Islam is the biggest growing religion in the world, and it will continue to be. And they always fearmonger their people, worrying about how Muslims are taking over. At the same time, they tell you Islam is dying. They just live in a delusion. But why are they saying that? Because this is their tactic. It's an infinite loop that we shouldn't be falling in as Muslims. What is this loop? The loop is: you debate them, and their fans go vote their comments; even though they lost, they will pretend they won. And then they will ask you to debate again. You don't debate them, they will say that you're scared and you're afraid. Even if you debated them before, they will use that excuse. So it's a never-ending loop. Whether you debate them or you don't debate them, it's going to be the same thing over and over again. You can go look at previous debates and look at comment section, depending on the channel. And then they ask for another debate, and the debate is repeated again.", "My biggest issue is this: they're debating the same topic over and over again. They make people think the topic that is being debated is actually a strong argument or a good topic. If some of the Muslims did not debate this nonsensical topic, because it's a nonsensical topic, nobody would think it matters, because it doesn't matter. Why entertain it? How can you debate a side issue, a branch issue, like this one that they're debating over and over again? I don't need to mention it. Muslims know what I'm talking about. It's a side point. It's not a main core of the doctrine, and they don't debate something else in the other person's belief. Aren't our purpose is to bring people to Islam? If I'm debating some sort of a side issue with a Christian where I'm only defending some side issue in Islam, how is that going to bring people closer to [Islam]? Can we not use our intellect and mind when we were engaging with these people?", "They're making hundreds of thousands of dollars lying to their audiences. It's a business. They don't care about Christianity. It's a business for views and counts. And sadly, on the Muslim side, we have a similar issue. We have Muslims who are chasing view count: let's debate so we can get views. I'm not going into anybody's heart or intentions, but some of them said this. They said, \"Yeah, we're chasing the views.\" So we have this serious issue where it's just about the [clout], it's just about the view count, it's just about repeatedly doing the same thing over and over again for entertainment, like we're in a coliseum and we have X person versus Z person.", "What is the main issues that we're seeing with this? First is that you're engaging with people who are vile, who are enemies of Islam, who don't care about the truth; they're insincere people. And you're engaging with them over and over again. And don't tell me it's about their audience, because the argument that you're engaging with, like I said, it's a branch issue that doesn't focus on the things that bring people closer to Islam. Why they go on Christian platforms and they give these Christian platforms views and fame and publicity? Not only are they [giving] the fame and publicity to the Islam hater, but they're also [giving] to their platforms. So people start thinking, \"Oh, this topic actually needs to be addressed,\" even though it's the silliest topic I've seen in my life. Even the Muslims themselves, they say this. But they still debate a ridiculous topic like this over and over again. It's just about engaging with the non-Muslims and giving them publicity.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “These Debates Are A Complete Disaster!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=veL65uoaiUY", ((0, "0:00–0:44"), (46, "0:46–1:30"), (90, "1:30–2:44"), (166, "2:46–3:56"), (236, "3:56–4:53"), (310, "5:10–5:54"), (354, "5:54–6:59"),), id: 3342)
// U ML018:161-289 | The Muslim Lantern | 8
#passage(("Some people came on talking about me: \"Oh, you've debated this person or you've debated that person.\" And they compare formal debates, where you come and you give this person time and that time, with someone who came on my platform; I didn't know who he was and I engaged with him. And it was a one-time engagement, and never engaged with them again. And they're trying to compare that with what they're doing, debating over and over again. And some of these people that are so friendly with the disbelievers in this way, they attack their Muslim brothers [day and night] for small issues. And those disbelievers that are like this with Allah and his messenger, they're being friendly with them.", "Engaging in debates, if there is a benefit, if you're doing it in the right way, if you're doing it with bringing people to Islam on important matters: I'm not telling you this is haram. This is allowed. We have examples for this in the Quran and Sunnah. You have to be qualified. The person you're engaging with, you bring him more benefit than harm. You're not engaging with these vile people that are not sincere, in side issues over and over again, where you're benefiting them. You're doing anti-dawah by giving them a platform, by giving them views.", "We have examples, like Abdullah ibn Abbas, when he debated the Khawarij. If we learn just from this interaction, if we do the debates or engagements the same way he did, you would see the huge benefit that will come upon this ummah. But we're not. So Abdullah ibn Abbas, he went to debate the Khawarij. He was wearing some of the best clothes from the clothing of the people of Yemen. When he came and he met them, the first thing they did is they attacked him: \"What is this kind of very expensive clothing that you're wearing?\" So how did he respond to them? They ad hominem attacked his personality. Muslims in these discussions, being attacked in their personalities, so they attack the person's personality. No, he said to them, \"Why are you blaming me? I've seen the messenger of Allah in the best of clothing, better than the one I'm wearing.\" Then he told them that Allah says in the Quran, \"Who forbid the [zinah] of Allah, the beautiful things that Allah have brought out to his people?\" That would be similar to me talking to a Christian who's insulting the Muslim clothing and then telling him, \"What did Jesus wear, for example?\" So you bring them something that they value. You tell them, \"Why are you criticizing something that you yourself are supposed to value?\"", "Then he said, \"I came to you from the company of the messenger of Allah, the Sahabah, people who the Quran came down upon them, and they were the most knowledgeable.\" So he's establishing that he's a qualified person. He knows what he's talking about. He's a person of knowledge. He's coming from people of knowledge. And he says to them, \"And among you there is none of them.\" So he's saying to them, \"If you are upon the truth, you would have the knowledgeable people among you. You wouldn't be a bunch of ignorant people sitting down. You'd have knowledgeable people, people who had the Quran sent down upon them, sitting with you.\"", "Then he asked them what their issues are with Ali ibn Abi Talib. They said they have three issues. First, they said that he used men to judge, and judgment is only for Allah, because Allah says in the Quran, \"In al-hukmu illa lillah.\" Judgment is only for Allah. And when he fought against Aisha, he didn't take any captives. He didn't take any bounty. So if the people he was fighting were disbelievers, then he can take his bounty. And if they were believers, then he shouldn't have fought them. And they said that he deleted in writing his name \"Amir al-Mu'minin\", the leader of the believers; then he is the leader of the disbelievers. So then Abdullah ibn Abbas said, \"Do you have anything else?\" Look how Abdullah ibn Abbas, he approaches these people. He says, \"What are your issues? What is your rational issues? Let's focus on the problems that you have, so we can solve these problems that you have and move into something productive.\"", "Then he addressed their issues. He said, \"You only say judgment is for Allah? Allah says in the Quran, 'O you who believe, do not do hunting while you are in ihram.' And then Allah says, 'Then one trustworthy honest person among you will judge.' So Allah made the judgment for men.\" He also mentioned to them in the Quran, in chapter 4, where Allah says that if a husband and wife are having issues, then bring a judge from her family and from his family. So he says to them, \"Look, Allah is using men.\" Then he said to them, \"Is this sufficient?\" Then they said, \"Yes.\" So he's engaging with the points. He's responding to it with evidences, and they are submitting. Then he went to the second point. He said Allah says in the Quran that Aisha is from the mothers of the believers: \"The wives of the prophet are their mothers.\" So he said, \"Do you want us to take our mother as a captive?\" So he told them, \"Is this sufficient?\" They said, \"This is sufficient.\" And then he came to the last point, and he brought them the example of the Prophet himself, when he was in Sulhul Hudaibiyah, when the disbelievers of Quraish told the Prophet to remove that he's the messenger of Allah, because they said, \"If we believed you're a messenger of Allah, we wouldn't fight you.\" So the Prophet told Ali himself to remove \"Rasulullah\". So it was removed. He said to them, \"If you believe that Ali ibn Abi Talib is the leader of the disbelievers because he removed 'the leader of the believers', Prophet removed 'the messenger of Allah', and that did not make him not the messenger of Allah.\" And then a third of them came back with him.", "This was a discussion and engagement and a debate Abdullah ibn Abbas had with people. However, look at the mannerisms: focusing on the topic, focusing on the issues that they had, addressing them directly with evidences, and then making sure that they accepted it, and then coming back with people who are guided. That is the purpose behind doing it. It's not entertainment.", "So we honestly need to fix this issue. You need to stop clicking on this stuff. You need to stop supporting this stuff. I'm not saying don't debate, don't engage with people. Engage with people, but engage with people who are civil, people who are not known it's a business for them to insult Allah and his messenger day and night. This is not the type of people you should be engaging with. This is not the type of people you should be promoting. And if the Muslims are doing this, then you need to call them out. And it's not just about a Muslim saying, \"Oh, I admit, yes, I'm doing this wrong,\" and then he goes back and doing the same thing again and again. Some people come out and say, \"Yeah, I agree. I'm doing this wrong. Inshallah, I will be better.\" And then he continues to do it. Then he needs to be continuously called out until they stop doing these type of things. If you continue clicking on it and giving it views and fame, then the people will continue to do it. You're responsible for making these Islamophobes famous. You're responsible for the money that they take and they steal from the people without right. You're responsible for the evil that they're spreading among people, because you are promoting them over and over again continuously.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “These Debates Are A Complete Disaster!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=veL65uoaiUY", ((541, "9:01–9:32"), (576, "9:36–10:02"), (607, "10:07–11:17"), (680, "11:20–11:47"), (707, "11:47–12:31"), (751, "12:31–13:52"), (832, "13:52–14:13"), (858, "14:18–15:52"),), id: 3343)
// U ML060:1576-1649 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("I think the first thing is you give too much energy to the wrong things. You give too much energy to the wrong people. There's a lot of people who are trying to rage bait you, get you to spend your energy talking about them. I didn't watch, but I saw you had maybe [a] discussion, like two and a half hours, with this guy. This is a complete waste of time. This guy probably looking for fame and publicity, and you don't need to take that long. And this is a tactic: drain your energy, get you to work too much. And there's so many bots out there as well, actual bots on Twitter, and I don't respond to them for that reason, because you got a tweet and you got like 500 likes instantly, because they're all bots, and they're all commenting the same thing, and they cover up what you want to say. You cannot give energy to the wrong people. These two hours you could have been talking about something a lot more important, a lot more influential, or reserving this energy for the next day, for your own self.", "Second thing is don't forget your spiritual life. You might think, okay, everything is happening here, I'm going to talk about this, very important. But if you yourself start to compromise your spirituality, your connection with God, then you are losing the battle. Why do they choose the month of Ramadan to do all of these things? They always choose Ramadan. It's not the first time. They always attack on Ramadan, because they know it's a spiritual time. It's the most holy time. So stay on your spiritual thing.", "Another thing I'll say is sometimes you fall into basic debate tactics. I saw one of the clips from this dude: he was arguing with you about the definition. Who cares what the definition is? Nobody cares. It's war. It's all semantics. You cannot fall for the tricks and games. What they do is they always play the game: run away from the important point; let's stick on semantics, this word, that word.", "A lot of people want to criticize you. They need to look at what your intention is as well. You're trying to bring people together. It's not just about criticism and overlooking someone's great work. You can give someone advice in the right way, not just attacking him or being angry and screaming. This is not the right way to do things. You cannot confuse this with the idea of: I'm doing this work, and this work is more important than dawa. No, that work is a form of dawa as well, because when you are talking to these people, waking them up about the reality of the world, who's controlling things, what's going on, who the real enemy is, people will start reflect.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Sneako Questions The Muslim Lantern On Shia & Sunni Unity | The Iran War And More | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kdFNc6Jr6OQ", ((4495, "1:14:55–1:16:07"), (4568, "1:16:08–1:16:38"), (4601, "1:16:41–1:17:11"), (4663, "1:17:43–1:18:30"),), id: 3344)
// U ML263:669-697 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("People who are laymen, I don't believe they should be debating these issues in your comment section. That doesn't go anywhere most of the time. The people need to study for themselves; they need to do research themselves. What I'm asking everyone watching is to do the research.", "There's a difference between Da'wah and debate. If people watch my content, they will understand the difference between the two. There are people putting up a shield, who are not really interested in the Truth to begin with; those people, I don't engage with them. I engage with people who are actually doing the research. So they would ask questions, and I will give them responses. This is how my interaction is usually with people. And it's friendly, it's respectful. I do not curse anyone. I do not speak bad about anyone. I've been doing this for years; I've not had any negative encounters with anyone.", "As the Bible says (John8:32), \"seek the Truth and the Truth shall set you free.\" So you should follow your Bible and seek the Truth! And it says, \"Come let us reason together\" (Isaiah1:18). So we come together in reasoning, and through reasoning we reach the Truth!",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The Muslim Lantern Gets Sneak∅ To Rethink His Views! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ocg_ABofNVU", ((2012, "33:32–33:46"), (2062, "34:22–34:48"), (2094, "34:54–35:01"),), id: 3345)

=== 66.17 Researching and answering anti-Islam arguments
// NODE P15.C03.S17
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR163:1-208 | Farid Responds | 7
#passage(("By watching this you should be able to figure out how to deal with the arguments that are brought up against Islam, not only by opponents of Islam and critics of Islam, but also by heretical sects, by all forms of [zanadiqa].", "What inspired me is, I was watching a series of videos by a heretic that was duping Muslims into believing some of the craziest theories, and while watching that I came to the conclusion that there's a major issue with how we're supposed to deal with the sources and how we're supposed to approach information. So here's the thing: [opponents] of Islam and heretics, they come with a lot of material that has little to no substance, but they speak a lot, and they sound confident, and due to that people are unfortunately duped into swallowing what they toss at them. And hey, this is your [deen], and if someone's gonna give you information to consume, you need to start chewing. You need to put in the work, you need to do the research. Providing answers is a solution, but it's not the best solution. I want you guys to do the work yourself, think for yourself. I'm not giving you a fish; I'm teaching you how to fish.", "The sections covered are as follows: primary sources, demanding one, authenticity, the authoritativeness of sources; all that stuff has to do with the [isnad]. Now comes the [matn]: reading comprehension, classical definitions, and dealing with those that are pretending to respond but they're not really responding. Just by keeping these few things in mind, you will be able to deal with over 90 percent of the stuff that's tossed at you.", "In Islamic literature, the main thing that we deal with, the [hujjah] upon us, what's binding upon us: a primary source. Primary source you're familiar with: it's the Quran, the Sunnah, the early classical collections of hadith, Bukhari and Muslim and whatnot. If someone's going to make a claim against Islam: making a claim is the easiest thing to do in the world. All you got to do is just make a claim. But if you want to prove something, then you have to go out of your way and do the research, and the burden of proof is upon you to show this content from some sort of primary reference. \"Oh, did you know that Muhammad, peace be upon him, used to worship Jesus?\" Really? Do you have a primary source that states that? \"Oh, did you know that your prophet allowed cannibalism?\" Really? Well, do you have a source for that claim? And just by demanding a source you often end the discussion, because most people are too lazy to do the research.", "So often you'll find people quoting secondary sources instead of primary sources, which is strange if the primary source is available. But what's the difference between a primary source and a secondary source? Generally, primary sources are early sources; they usually include the chain all the way up to [Rasulullah], and that's what's binding. A secondary source is often quite late and often is quoting a primary source. Sometimes it's just attributing a statement to the prophet, peace be upon him, without a chain. So secondary source is not proper evidence.", "I can think of three main reasons as to why someone would use a secondary source instead of a primary source. The first reason why they will use a secondary source instead of a primary source is because a secondary source may include a mistranslation or a typo or a mistake somewhere, and that information can be used against Islam, so they're going to make use of it. Why is he quoting an article on a website instead of quoting the [primary source] itself? Why isn't he showing a screenshot from one of those books that he's quoting from? Well, it's because the highlighted sections can't be found in the books that he's quoting. However, by relying on this website, which seems to have some sort of botched translation, he's able to use this evidence against Muslims, and he's like, yo, check it out, it's a Muslim site. Another reason why someone will quote a secondary source instead of a primary source is to overburden you with evidence, so much evidence that you're not gonna know how to deal with it.", "There are a number of reasons why secondary sources don't really add much more to the conversation. Here's a quick example. Imagine a mother has a cookie in a cookie jar, and she says, nobody eat this cookie. She leaves the cookie on the table; they all go to bed. The next morning, the cookie's gone. The mom gathers the family. She says, who ate the cookie? So the boy says, I saw the dog eat the cookie. The father says, well, my boy said the dog ate the cookie. The grandmother said the boy said the dog ate the cookie. The sister said the boy said that he saw the dog eat the cookie. So these are four testimonies; however, there's only one eyewitness. So in reality we're not dealing with four primary sources; we're dealing with one, and it's the boy. The other three don't add anything more to the statement of the boy; they're just quoting him. These are not independent sources that strengthen one another. Ultimately, we're only dealing with one source. Now, I know that this example is quite simplistic; however, even some of the most dedicated people against Islam don't even understand these basics.",), "Farid Responds, “Refutations 101 [Crash Course]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CgfiwsD4Sns", ((6, "0:06–0:25"), (25, "0:25–1:39"), (125, "2:05–2:39"), (189, "3:09–4:05"), (248, "4:08–4:38"), (278, "4:38–6:05"), (365, "6:05–7:30"),), id: 3346)
// U FR163:394-460 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("Another aspect to keep in mind, which is related to the previous two points, is authority, and I'm of course referring to the authority of sources. Is the person that's being used as evidence, is this person [an] authority in his field or not? For example, modern historians sometimes use kitab [al-Imama wal-Siyasa]; however, we don't know who the actual author of the book is. That's why you'll find some other modern historians referring to him as [pseudo] ibn [Qutaybah] instead of ibn [Qutaybah] himself.", "You see, some people, they don't even know what they're quoting. They don't even know the title of the book; they don't know who the author is; they don't know if this person is an authoritative source or not. However, they're just going to quote anything that's in a book, because they think everything's authoritative. By the way, this doesn't just apply to religious science; it applies to all works of non-fiction.", "So who is this DD that's speaking about his own patients? Well, it turns out that DD is just a pen name, and we have no idea who this person actually is. Now, he claims to be someone with a degree in religion and someone that's a certified neurologist, but what's the proof of that? At the end of the day, it's just his claim and no one else's. And are we going to trust someone's claim that he's an authority, when the only proof that he's an authority is his own claim? Well, that's not really how it works.",), "Farid Responds, “Refutations 101 [Crash Course]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CgfiwsD4Sns", ((930, "15:30–16:00"), (999, "16:39–17:02"), (1056, "17:36–18:09"),), id: 3347)
