#passage(("They're talking about [Abu Layth], and they're talking about sitting down with [Abu Layth] and having a discussion with them in order to solve their differences and to fix things and to have a cordial conversation.", "When you sit on a table with alcohol, there are a number of things that are happening. Well, first of all, there is a possibility of you being affected. There's a possibility of you belittling the act: when you're away, you look at alcohol in the most negative way, but when you're sitting with people drinking, you're like: well, okay, it's not that bad, they're not acting that badly, so maybe it's not that big of a sin. And thirdly, what about the people that are looking at you? To them, you as a religious person being there legitimizes their sin, legitimizes their drinking of the alcohol.", "So connect that with sitting with [Abu Layth]. Indeed, it's the exact same thing. You may be affected by the [shubhat], you may be affected by what he says, you may be belittling what he says, because he's saying it to you in a sweet way, in a nice way, and the others that are watching, they're going to be like: oh, okay, so this legitimizes this guy's views in a way; if it was that big of a deal, they wouldn't be sitting with him. That's the reality.",), "Farid Responds, “A Message to Dawah Men”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K-nDaLYskuo", ((18, "0:18–0:33"), (131, "2:11–3:00"), (182, "3:02–3:36"),), id: 3356)
// U FR226:118-170 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("This isn't just about [Abu Layth]. I've actually contacted people who have sat with [Abu Layth], and a bunch of them were not even aware of his views; some of them are aware but don't care, and I'm hoping most of them are in the former category, because anyone who sits down with [Abu Layth] gives him legitimacy. Of course he will receive legitimacy from the murtadin community and the islamophobic community, because he does have guests; there are islamophobes. But I think we're talking about Muslim, we're talking about people that represent Islam, and they're going on this show. Insha allah they don't know what he's saying; inshallah they're not aware of the blasphemy that he spews.", "If you have been on a platform with them, it becomes harder to speak against them when they cross the line. You won't be able to speak against them, you won't be able to condemn them, you won't be able to say anything, because you've already built that connection. And this is a message to the duat; it's not a message to the average youtube viewer, the average muslim. This is to the [duat] and to the [mashayikh] who are sitting next to people that spew kuffur, and they sit there silently: \"Masha'allah, take the good from him.\" And you want to share a platform with him? As [Maulana Uthman] said, it's worse than sitting on a table that serves alcohol, because that's clear: people know that this is haram. But they don't know that this is kufur; they're not aware this is kufur. This is a [shubhah].",), "Farid Responds, “A Message to Dawah Men”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K-nDaLYskuo", ((333, "5:33–6:27"), (387, "6:27–7:31"),), id: 3357)
// U FR277:1-88 | Farid Responds | 7
#passage(("This is a message to all the [dawah] channels on YouTube. Defending Islam from the attacks of atheists, ex-Muslims, Christians, it's not really my thing. It's not really my field. But the reason why I'm here, the reason why I'm crunching out all these videos, is because I realized about a year ago that there aren't many people doing that, which is really, really strange. There are many, many [Dawah] channels out there, and yet it seems like a lot of them are focused on a lot of other things.", "Now, I do understand that apologetics isn't the most important thing in the religion. I do understand that a lot of channels focus on Quran related studies. They focus on Arabic teaching, which is great, and reminders. That's great. I appreciate it. What irritates me, though, is the focus, the amount of attention that's given to refuting other Muslims. Now, yes, of course, I'm aware that sometimes there are Muslims that deserve to be refuted. I'm aware that sometimes people make really problematic statements, statements that are bordering kufur, and they need to be shot down. They need to be refuted thoroughly. I understand that. However, I'm not talking about that. I'm talking about some issues that don't deserve this attention, because there are priorities.", "There are several channels that are really doing well, that are getting hundreds of thousands of views per video attacking Islam, and no one's doing anything about it. Let's be frank. For example, David Wood started making videos like 10 years ago. Now he's got 1,200 videos on his channel or something like that. He's making like one or two videos per day. What are you guys doing about it? No one's doing jack. Instead, we have a lot of video [refutations] against cults. I appreciate the concern in regards to guiding. I do appreciate that. Priorities.", "This is not my field, and I'm doing this out of necessity. And the thing is, you guys have [ilm] for this. You guys go really deep into [shubuhat], of course shubhat in regards to matters that you disagree with other Sunnis. However, when it comes to [shubuhat] brought by non-Muslims, you just ignore it. Apostasy is a real thing. And I didn't know this. Maybe you don't know. Even though you're living in the west, I'm living in the Middle East, but maybe you don't know, which is strange, but this is a reality. I don't get why this is being ignored.", "And subhan Allah, a lot of them are very sincere folk. A lot of them are extremely sincere. It's not some sort of disease of the heart for every single person. A lot of ex-Muslims just want an answer. Some guy I was talking to a while ago, he was like: \"I saw these attacks against Islam and I just didn't find an answer.\" No one's putting anything out. There are arguments made against the [deen] with no responses out there. Yeah, you can find them, if you dig deep enough, third page into Google, you might find an article, a short article or something, but there are no videos, and we are in the video age.", "So what am I supposed to do? Am I supposed to make videos against every single Islamophobe, Islam critic out there, wait for you guys to catch up? Sometimes I think to myself: is it that I care more? I don't think I care more. Sometimes I don't know what drives me. But to be honest, this attitude, this negligence, it's a major issue, and you guys need to start stepping up.", "So this is a plea, not to you, because a lot of you guys don't care, but this is a plea to the viewers who really actually do care, who really care strongly about this stuff. If you see your favorite dawa channel attacking another [dawah] channel, get my message out to those guys. Tell them that we have priorities that we need to focus on, and the priorities isn't refuting this and that guy and this cult.",), "Farid Responds, “Dear Dawah Channel”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4pljZhgm9tQ", ((3, "0:03–0:56"), (56, "0:56–2:01"), (124, "2:04–3:42"), (226, "3:46–4:23"), (263, "4:23–5:09"), (309, "5:09–5:59"), (359, "5:59–7:01"),), id: 3358)

