// U ML053:262-292 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("I used to work in an environment where you work with other people. That was many years ago, and at that time I was still reading the Bible. So I had it, and we would be in the break, or your eating place where you're eating food, and I would be reading while I'm eating. And there were Muslim co-workers; they would look at me so weird, like: what are you doing? Why are you reading the Bible? You're supposed to be a Muslim. Why are you reading this book? Of course, I'm reading it for the purposes of research and understanding and all that, but they didn't understand. And some people were so suspicious as well: \"Are you Christian?\" I'm like, no, I'm reading the Bible. I'm just seeing what this scripture says. So I had a very similar, miniature experience.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Arab Christian Shares His Experience With Islam And Christianity! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kEUpYAh5b3E", ((821, "13:41–14:26"),), id: 3483)
// U ML318:723-837 | The Muslim Lantern | 9
#passage(("If you realize Islam is the Truth, then you would want to tell your family that Islam is the Truth, because you want good for them in the Afterlife. If something would make them upset and make them angry even if it's good for them, then is it good to hide it from them, and allow them to die on something which is not true?", "Yes, that happens with the people who accept Islam. When they accept Islam they come across this experience that you're mentioning. They come across their parents IN THE BEGINNING, and that's the key point here. Not all of them: there are many people who are from, especially as you said, secularism; they just don't care what religion anyone follows. But there are certain parents who do actually follow the religion, so they would not like that you accepted a different religion. But still my point stands. It's not about what they think is right or wrong. We're saying if you already now established, you accept Islam because you know it's true, now it's not about what anyone thinks. So you know Islam is the truth and you know they will be disappointed by you telling them about the Truth; then is it a better alternative to allow them to die on this belief? Or is the true love to tell them that which is True even if it will hurt them? True love is to say the Truth even if it goes against you, even if it will hurt someone's feelings.", "\"Not saying something\" is not the same as \"saying the opposite of something\". If I accept Islam and I practice Islam and then the topic doesn't come up, they don't ask me if you left Christianity, and then I don't mention it, it doesn't mean I'm lying! If they ask you, are you a Muslim? and you say No, I'm not! then this is lying, I would agree with you. But in the beginning of the journey, what a lot of reverts do -and I see it does work a lot with a lot of them- is that they accept Islam, they keep it to themselves in the beginning, until they practice Islam a little bit. They get established, they get familiar with the teachings of Islam, they get familiar with the community, because if their parents ask them questions they're also able to respond; they're not just new reverts who are not able to engage at all. And then some of them do it after 5 years, some of them after 2 years, some of them after 3 years.", "But in reality, is it true or not true; that's what matters. If something is true, then when I die my parents will not help me. I'm not gonna help my parents and my parents are not going to help me.", "Accepting the Truth is not gonna be easy. Islam doesn't tell you is always going to be rainbows and butterflies after you accept Islam. But what Islam says: that Islam is True and it is from God, and it says if you rely on Allah then Allah will be on your side (Quran65:3), doesn't matter even if the whole Earth is against you, and Allah will place contentment in your heart, and Allah can change the state of anyone. And I know people who accepted Islam: they lost their wives, they lost their children, they lost their job! But Allah replaced them better: a better wife, more children, a better work.", "This comes back to the same point I was mentioning: humans have a tendency to follow that which they found their forefathers upon. And you are saying the same thing! You're saying even if this is True, I'm gonna be with my family. I know this is difficult for you, and obviously it is difficult. I'm not a person who comes from a non-Muslim family, but I know many people who are. So I know it is a difficult thing, but I cannot sugarcoat the Truth for you! I can say to you that what really matters is the Afterlife, not this life. And that's why the Companions of the Prophetﷺ endured -some of them being murdered, being tortured for 13 years continuously. They endured it because they know even if I die there's an eternal life that is going to come. What matters is not how people perceive me in this life, what matters is not whether people are pleased with me or not in this life. What matters is what's going to happen to me when I die: am I going to end up in Paradise? or am I going to end up in Hellfire? That is the ONLY thing that really matters.", "So what I would advise is this: if Islam is True, you rely on Allahﷻ, you rely on the Creator, and you try with your parents. You don't necessarily have to say to them Islam is True. You might try with them, which a lot of people do: you ask them questions about Islam here and there, and you try to see their perception, and then if they say something you say OK, but I heard this and that, and give answers to these questions, so gradually changing their perception of what Islam is. And many people I know -most of the people I know- once they accept Islam and they tell their parents, in the end their parents accept Islam, because Human Beings are rational.", "One of the people I know: his parents kicked him out of the house and they said we never want to speak to you again! But then in the end they accepted Islam, but it was later, 15 years later; it was after work, him trying to get back to them, trying to speak to them gradually, and then gradually inviting them. Things in life happen step by step. If someone lived all his life upon something, it's not easy for him just to change like this. It takes time! So things take time, especially when people have a lot of misconceptions about a specific faith like Islam, very popular misconceptions about Islam in America. But Truth is the Truth, and God is the Ever-Living, the All-Powerful, which means He is the One I want on my side, no other human being that I want on my side.", "The people at the time of Prophet Muhammedﷺ, they accepted Islam, their families were not Muslims. His companions, when they accepted Islam, their family was the ones who were against them, they were the ones who were trying to kill them. But again, if you pray to Allah, you pray to the Creator, you pray to God, and you ask God for Guidance, and you ask God to settle the Truth in your heart, He's able to do it. I cannot do anything except tell you the Truth and give you reasons why is the Truth. I can't change the heart for anyone.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Lady Enquires About Heaven In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NyrpGRsef5I", ((1909, "31:49–32:01"), (1934, "32:14–33:04"), (1997, "33:17–33:53"), (2038, "33:58–34:04"), (2060, "34:20–34:43"), (2098, "34:58–35:49"), (2151, "35:51–36:20"), (2183, "36:23–37:06"), (2230, "37:10–37:35"),), id: 3484)
