#passage(("A person from the IOF came on the live stream. This is available online for people to watch, still available online. People usually, when they join my live stream, they are in the backstage. There's [an] area called The Backstage, and then I can bring them into the stream, and I can see their name and video if they have the video on. And he looked extremely unhappy, because he's taking medications, medicated, etc., and I was like: okay, this is going to be interesting conversation, to say the least.", "He came on. First thing, he asked me about Hamas: do you support Hamas, and this and that. So he started on the more of the other side, offensive kind of narrative, the normal narrative that they are fed by the media, and a lot of Americans are as well fed by the media. We started responding, saying actually this land was taken. He said: are you okay with killing innocent people? We said no, we condemn killing innocent people done by any party, whether it's done by a Muslim, whether it's done by a non-muslim. We are not with killing innocent people. In Islam, even in war, you cannot kill children, you cannot kill women, monks, priests; destroying places of worship, crops, all of that is explicitly prohibited in the Islamic text. The prophet did that: whenever he appointed an army leader, he commanded him, said to him: you cannot do any of these things. And Abu Bakr did the same thing, and the companions of the Prophet sallallahu alaihi wasallam. So I said to him, we condemn all of that.", "And then gradually we started talking about it, and then he started mentioning his experience of the oppression, what he've seen, what the other soldiers have done, with his own eyes, [firsthand] testimony. He's saying things that he did himself too. He is saying that we held guns to a young boy, and he saw the boy urinating himself in front of his own eyes. And he's talking about the trauma he's having. Imagine a trauma the kid have! So he's having a trauma because he's doing something so oppressive to a child that he knows a child is helpless, and they were laughing. He's saying him and the rest of the soldiers were laughing. So he's having a trauma, knowing deep down his [fitrah] is killing him, the conscience that he has is killing him.", "That's what you see: Allah has different ways to punish the people. It's not just as simple as physical direct punishment that you can punish someone. The psychological pain, I think I said that to him, is way more severe than any other pain, because it's not something you can switch off. Physically, you got a pain, you can get some sort of medication, or you can get some sort of a drug that moves you into a state in which you're numb, you don't feel your body parts anymore. But when it comes to psychological illness, psychological pain, no medication will help you. You can take as many medications as you want; they just make your situation get worse and worse and worse. So you see it here as well, predominant in the US: you go to the psychiatric or psychologist, whatever it is, and then he just lets you speak, speak, speak, speak. Take this medication, go spend some money for pharmaceutical companies, come back again, speak, speak, more medication. You just become more miserable upon misery upon misery, and more money, more medication, more money for the pharmaceutical companies, no actual effects, no change of condition, cuz you're not dealing with the problem. They're taught not to enforce anything, reinforce anything; they just listen. So how is that going to solve anything?", "He started speaking about his experiences, how this is influencing him, and he was almost crying in the Stream. And he even opened his cupboard; he had the medication inside, and he's bringing out, he's saying: look, this is Xanax, or I don't know, this is this, this is that. And he's saying all of these soldiers take these things. He's not saying just me, like a special case situation; he's saying all of us. Because what we don't see is terrifying, what happens to the Palestinians. You see a lot of things and you feel pain, but what you don't see is far greater: what these people do without a camera, because they're alone in the house or there's no one filming. And sometimes for fun they film, and you see some of these clips. Because of how psychologically deranged [they] are, and because of all of the medication become even more psychologically deranged, they themselves, out of fun, they film, and these things get leaked, and then they are on social media. But what we don't see, what we don't encounter, what happens to our brothers and sisters, is far greater.", "So it was just a step by step, and he was saying how much he's suffering. And I want to comment on this: even though I'm saying all of this, Allah is Forgiven and [Merciful]. If it [was] up to us, we'll never forgive him. Until this day, comments of Muslims: no, not going to accept you. You're not the one who accepts; it's Allah who accepts the repentance. He's the one who accepts, not you. If it was up to you, probably no one would be guided. So even though he's done all of these things, even though he has left the sinful life, I told him that Allah is a forgiven and merciful god. Allah says in the Quran: say, oh my servants [who've been excessive] in doing sins and transgression and disobedience, Allah says, do not despair of the mercy. Allah forgives all the sins. He's the forgiven, the merciful. Return to your lord and submit to him before the day comes, the day of judgment.", "I told him some of the verses, explaining to him that you have a chance, you have an opportunity, you're still alive. And you speaking out, I told him, it's one of the ways of you trying to rectify some of what you've done, because you're clarifying to the world: one of the IOF soldiers here, standing with you, telling you firsthand experience, what he did and all of that, what he's done to the children, he've seen with his eyes people are doing to the children, to the women, to the Palestinians, and how much these people are medicated or taking all of this medication in order for them to kind of try to pretend as if there's nothing wrong.", "So he ended up saying he want to accept Islam. This was, and I said this before, the most unexpected encounter that I had so far on a live stream, alhamdulillah. And he accepted Islam. And when I messaged him after, he said something interesting: it is good that the world can see this. He said that because I said to him, I'm worried about you as well, because his friends, he was saying, are all from that land: all my friends are going to hate me. Of course, he's been living all his life that way. So I was worried about him. I sent him an email after. I said: are you okay? Is everything good? Give me details, and try to connect if you need any help. But he said to me: no, no, no, it's good that the people can see. Because he understands it's good that people can actually see what's going on, people can actually see what is being done.", "And he said that: I would tell my friends; there's many of them that would want to talk to you. He said many of my friends, they're scared, obviously: scared first of the IOF forces, and their families, and the social pressure, and the government pressure, and all of that, and secondly scared of Muslims, because of the emotional response that they would give. If that man came to us, just random Muslim, probably start cursing him, just back and forth cursing, shouting.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Eddie @TheDeenShowTV Interviews The Muslim Lantern | Masjid Dar Al-Qur'an | Chicago - USA”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sLfJyJGj_eg", ((415, "6:55–7:32"), (457, "7:37–8:33"), (513, "8:33–9:25"), (567, "9:27–10:46"), (648, "10:48–12:00"), (720, "12:00–13:14"), (794, "13:14–13:56"), (836, "13:56–14:49"), (900, "15:00–15:32"),), id: 3504)

