#passage(("Christian Prince made the claim that marriage doesn't exist in Islam. A brother, One Moderate Muslim, tagged me to a tweet notifying me that the St Catherine's Monastery in Egypt had uploaded their collection of manuscripts, and one of the first manuscripts that I came across was the Gospel of Matthew, which was written seven centuries ago in the Arabic language, and guess what I found: [yankihu].", "So here is what I would like to know, and I'm sincerely hoping that our good friend Salibi can provide a clear answer to this question: why does a 13th century translation of the Gospel of Matthew include the word [yankihu]? Is this not sufficient evidence that the term [yankihu] means marriage according to both Muslims and Christian Arabs? Isn't this sufficient evidence that Christian Prince is being deceptive about the meaning of the word?",), "Farid Responds, “Christian Prince Lies about Bible (2021 re-upload)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eKUdkMrGPrM", ((0, "0:00–1:15"), (80, "1:20–1:56"),), id: 3733)
// U FR229:32-306 | Farid Responds | 6
#passage(("Yesterday I showed [Salibi] three images that show that Christians use the word nikah to mean marriage. This is significant because he claims that the word only means sex. I've proven this to be untrue with three images: one from the Bible, one from a book published by the Coptic Church and one from a Christian dictionary. Let's start with Salibi's response to the biblical verse that I quoted. Can you believe that he accused me of using a photoshopped image?", "First point I'd like to make is that this is not some random image of the internet. You can check it out yourself, and you can yourself see that this image isn't [photoshopped]. Salibi made the claim that this is the Van Dyke translation. Now this is another lie by him. Look at this note on the Facebook page that he's quoting: this is a translation. The author of the Facebook post is showing you two Arabic translations to show you that the meaning is the same. Salibi instead is claiming that this text is from the Van Dijk translation; that was a claim that was not made by the Facebook user. It says \"this translation\". So what is this translation? It is the translation that was printed in the year 1816, before the translation of the Van Dyke translation by decades. Did you forget your reading glasses, CP? This is basic Arabic. Or are you being deceptive here?", "Some of your followers are Arab Christians. Some of them are critical thinkers; some of them are quite smart. Sure, you are successful with your non-Arab audience, but why would you make such a bold claim like this? Do you want your Arab Christian audience to lose faith in you? Nobody is fabricating any images. Once again, you can go download the book yourself and check it out. Get an Arab Christian to help you out; let them read the verse in question for you. The word used in this Bible is [yankihu].", "Isn't it desperate to claim that Muslims are photoshopping Bibles in order to win an argument? I know that you hate Islam, but you don't have to lie to yourself. Just because Islam is false according to you doesn't mean that this image is photoshopped. You don't have to stick with this guy who's openly lying to you in your face and making you look bad. Move on, use someone else. There are a lot of Christian apologists out there that attack Islam; you've just chosen the worst one.", "There were three images that I shared. During one point in his live stream [Salibi] denies that there were any other images. He didn't want to deal with them. He's clearly avoiding the fact that I've shared three different images from three different books, thinking that his deflection will cause his audience to let it go. However, his audience persisted and demanded that he address the other images. He then finally gave in and responded to my claim. He tries to evade the matter with laughing; no real response. He says it's a choice and somebody wrote this. Remember what he said earlier: he said that there's no Christian that used the word nikah to mean marriage. I'd like to remind you once again: the third image that I quoted was from an Arabic French dictionary by a Christian linguist. Salibi ignored that and didn't address it.", "There are Christians that watch my videos, and some of them actually pay attention to what I have to say, and when they see you messing up so badly it kind of shakes their faith, because their faith is tied to what you're bringing forward.",), "Farid Responds, “Christian Prince says: THEY PHOTOSHOPPED THE BIBLE!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rmuWdRscO88", ((75, "1:15–1:45"), (257, "4:17–5:38"), (340, "5:40–6:10"), (375, "6:15–7:05"), (494, "8:14–11:05"), (685, "11:25–11:58"),), id: 3734)
